  "worked_solution": "A vector $u$ belongs to the kernel of a linear transformation $T$ if $T(u)=0.$ \n\nTherefore, we need to compute $T({v}_{i})$ for each vector ${v}_{i}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({v}_{1}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ -48\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{2}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ T({v}_{3}) & =T([\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}42 \\\\ -84\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\,T({v}_{2})=0,$ we conclude that the only vector that belongs to the kernel of $\\,T$ is $\\,{v}_{2}.$",
